1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,625
A CI job, an automated build,
prints its remotes for debugging.

2
00:00:08,665 --> 00:00:13,187
Someone copies the output into a
ticket to ask why the fetch is slow.

3
00:00:13,227 --> 00:00:18,674
In the middle of the line, between "https://"
and "@github.com", is a classic token

4
00:00:18,714 --> 00:00:23,897
with the repo scope. It was created two
years ago "for the changelog bot" by an

5
00:00:23,937 --> 00:00:25,748
engineer who has since left.

6
00:00:26,088 --> 00:00:29,852
Your CTO asks two things. How
far does this token reach?

7
00:00:29,892 --> 00:00:33,861
And how did a secret end up in
the output of git remote -v?

8
00:00:34,201 --> 00:00:38,969
The first answer is in the token's prefix.
The second is that somebody followed a

9
00:00:39,009 --> 00:00:43,539
tutorial and wrote the token into the
remote URL, which works, and that's the

10
00:00:43,579 --> 00:00:48,466
problem. Hold on to both questions. You'll
answer them for the changelog bot at the

11
00:00:48,506 --> 00:00:48,703
end.

12
00:00:50,543 --> 00:00:54,259
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:54,299 --> 00:00:59,247
In the last video the secret over HTTPS
was "a token", handed to Git by a helper.

14
00:00:59,287 --> 00:01:04,173
Now we look at the token itself: what
kinds exist, what each can reach, how long

15
00:01:04,213 --> 00:01:08,484
each lives, and what the first
characters tell you during an incident.

16
00:01:08,524 --> 00:01:12,548
Then one rule with a demonstration:
a token never goes into a URL.

17
00:01:12,888 --> 00:01:18,854
One replay, labs/ch16/token-in-url.sh. The
token in the script is a made-up string,

18
00:01:18,894 --> 00:01:24,570
FAKE-TOKEN-not-a-real-credential. There
is no GitHub walkthrough in this video:

19
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:26,958
token pages must not be recorded.

20
00:01:27,298 --> 00:01:31,955
The label for the practice we're about
to demonstrate comes from the chapter's

21
00:01:31,995 --> 00:01:36,893
safety table: writing a token into a URL,
a file or a command line is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

22
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:40,085
It publishes the secret to
logs, history and backups.

23
00:01:40,125 --> 00:01:44,722
There's no preview. And the recovery is
to revoke it, because cleaning is not

24
00:01:44,762 --> 00:01:47,251
enough. Twig looks worried, and fairly so.

25
00:01:49,091 --> 00:01:50,503
After this video you can:

26
00:01:50,843 --> 00:01:55,866
Compare fine-grained and classic tokens by
scope and lifetime, as the section gives

27
00:01:55,906 --> 00:01:56,171
them.

28
00:01:56,511 --> 00:01:59,439
Say when a documented gap
forces a classic token.

29
00:01:59,779 --> 00:02:02,863
Name the places a token
in a remote URL ends up.

30
00:02:03,203 --> 00:02:06,358
Find and repair a remote URL
that contains a credential.

31
00:02:08,198 --> 00:02:13,204
In one sentence: a token is a string that
stands for an account with a subset of its

32
00:02:13,244 --> 00:02:17,770
rights. The prefix says what kind it
is, and the kind decides how far a leak

33
00:02:17,810 --> 00:02:18,251
reaches.

34
00:02:21,591 --> 00:02:27,075
A personal access token, classic. Prefix
ghp_. Long-lived, and no expiry is

35
00:02:27,115 --> 00:02:32,820
required. It belongs to a user and reaches
by scope, such as repo or read:org,

36
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:37,239
across every repository and
organization the user can reach.

37
00:02:37,579 --> 00:02:41,394
A fine-grained personal access
token. Prefix github_pat_.

38
00:02:41,434 --> 00:02:44,978
Its lifetime is configurable,
up to one year or none.

39
00:02:45,018 --> 00:02:50,524
It belongs to a user and reaches one owner,
a user or one organization, optionally

40
00:02:50,564 --> 00:02:54,379
selected repositories, with
per-permission read or write.

41
00:02:54,719 --> 00:03:00,859
An OAuth app token. Prefix gho_. It belongs
to a user, through an app, and reaches

42
00:03:00,899 --> 00:03:07,114
by scope. gh auth login produces one.
Expiring tokens are the default for new OAuth

43
00:03:07,154 --> 00:03:10,204
apps since the fourteenth of August 2026.

44
00:03:10,544 --> 00:03:16,018
A GitHub App user token. A GitHub App is
an integration that acts with its own

45
00:03:16,058 --> 00:03:21,463
identity. Prefix ghu_. Eight hours, with
a refresh token, prefix ghr_, of six

46
00:03:21,503 --> 00:03:21,957
months.

47
00:03:22,297 --> 00:03:25,800
A GitHub App installation
token. Prefix ghs_. One hour.

48
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:31,017
It belongs to an app installation and
reaches the repositories and permissions of

49
00:03:31,057 --> 00:03:36,105
that installation. The GITHUB_TOKEN in a
workflow is an installation token that

50
00:03:36,145 --> 00:03:39,004
lives for the job and
reaches one repository.

51
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:43,750
Quick quiz. You find a token in
a log, and it starts with ghp_.

52
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:48,620
Whose access is it: one job's, one
app installation's, or a person's?

53
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:49,749
Say it out loud.

54
00:03:54,589 --> 00:03:59,302
A person's. So during an incident,
the prefix is your first fact.

55
00:03:59,342 --> 00:04:05,080
ghp_ means a person's access, everywhere
that person can go, possibly for ever.

56
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:08,005
ghs_ means one hour of one installation.

57
00:04:08,345 --> 00:04:13,126
GitHub recommends fine-grained tokens
over classic ones "whenever possible".

58
00:04:13,166 --> 00:04:17,567
They have been generally available
since the eighteenth of March 2025.

59
00:04:17,607 --> 00:04:22,198
By default an organization owner must
approve one before it can reach the

60
00:04:22,238 --> 00:04:26,703
organization's resources. Until then
it can read only public resources.

61
00:04:26,743 --> 00:04:29,177
An account can hold at most 50 of them.

62
00:04:29,517 --> 00:04:33,611
The documented gaps decide when
a classic token is still needed.

63
00:04:33,651 --> 00:04:38,714
A fine-grained token can't contribute to
public repositories where you aren't a

64
00:04:38,754 --> 00:04:43,429
member, or act for you as an outside
collaborator. It can't reach several

65
00:04:43,469 --> 00:04:48,597
organizations at once. It can't access
Packages, including docker login ghcr.io.

66
00:04:48,637 --> 00:04:52,731
And it can't call the Checks API,
or a few other REST endpoints.

67
00:04:52,771 --> 00:04:57,834
For open-source contribution that rules
fine-grained tokens out, and the answer

68
00:04:57,874 --> 00:05:03,002
there is the browser login of gh or an
SSH key, not a classic token typed into a

69
00:05:03,042 --> 00:05:03,454
prompt.

70
00:05:03,794 --> 00:05:08,668
Expiry and revocation. A token found in
a public repository or gist is revoked

71
00:05:08,708 --> 00:05:13,141
automatically. Personal and OAuth
tokens unused for a year are removed.

72
00:05:13,181 --> 00:05:18,433
Anyone who holds a leaked token value can
submit it to an unauthenticated revocation

73
00:05:18,473 --> 00:05:23,410
API. And organizations can restrict each
token type and set a maximum lifetime.

74
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:28,732
A version note for people who write
tooling: newly minted installation tokens,

75
00:05:28,772 --> 00:05:33,754
including the Actions GITHUB_TOKEN, use
a format of about 520 characters, in a

76
00:05:33,794 --> 00:05:37,167
staged rollout from the
twenty-seventh of April 2026.

77
00:05:37,207 --> 00:05:42,318
Older code assumed 40 characters. Never
validate a token by length or pattern in

78
00:05:42,358 --> 00:05:43,219
your own code.

79
00:05:43,559 --> 00:05:50,289
Now the rule. A URL of the form
https://USER:TOKEN@github.com/OWNER/REPO.git works,

80
00:05:50,329 --> 00:05:56,162
which is the problem. Why is it a
problem? The URL is configuration, and

81
00:05:56,202 --> 00:06:02,769
configuration isn't a secret store. The
token is then in a plain text file inside

82
00:06:02,809 --> 00:06:04,808
the repository directory.

83
00:06:05,148 --> 00:06:10,325
It's printed by git remote -v and by git
config list, which people paste into

84
00:06:10,365 --> 00:06:15,136
tickets and CI logs. And it's copied
by every backup of that directory.

85
00:06:15,176 --> 00:06:20,691
That's four places: the file .git/config,
the output of two everyday commands, the

86
00:06:20,731 --> 00:06:24,553
logs and tickets that output
is pasted into, and backups.

87
00:06:24,893 --> 00:06:30,955
Git can be told to refuse such URLs.
transfer.credentialsInUrl takes allow, which is

88
00:06:30,995 --> 00:06:36,330
the default, warn or die. The setting
exists since Git 2.37, and it covers

89
00:06:36,370 --> 00:06:42,140
remote.<name>.url and not pushurl. The
recommendation is git config set --global

90
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,319
transfer.credentialsInUrl die.

91
00:06:44,659 --> 00:06:48,332
The recovery has two halves,
and people forget the second.

92
00:06:48,372 --> 00:06:53,709
Clean the URL. And revoke the token. Erasing
isn't revoking: removing a token from a

93
00:06:53,749 --> 00:06:57,614
URL, from the keychain or from
a file leaves the token valid.

94
00:06:57,654 --> 00:06:59,278
Only GitHub can revoke it.

95
00:06:59,618 --> 00:07:04,824
The same reasoning excludes every other
place where a token ends up in plain sight.

96
00:07:04,864 --> 00:07:08,301
A command line, because ps
and shell history record it.

97
00:07:08,341 --> 00:07:13,294
An environment file under version control.
A .netrc in a home directory that is

98
00:07:13,334 --> 00:07:18,414
backed up. Hand a token to a program on
standard input, and let a helper keep it.

99
00:07:20,254 --> 00:07:25,227
Think of a token as a copy of your office
key card that opens only some doors and

100
00:07:25,267 --> 00:07:30,178
stops working at a set time. Two questions
size up a lost card: which doors, and

101
00:07:30,218 --> 00:07:35,005
until when. The prefix answers "which
kind of card", and the kind answers both

102
00:07:35,045 --> 00:07:35,624
questions.

103
00:07:35,964 --> 00:07:40,730
The model breaks in one place that matters:
a lost key card has to be found by

104
00:07:40,770 --> 00:07:45,660
someone who walks to your building. A
token works from anywhere, the moment it's

105
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:49,912
read. So "it was only in a log for
an hour" is not a mitigating fact.

106
00:07:49,952 --> 00:07:53,054
The only fact that ends the
exposure is revocation.

107
00:07:53,394 --> 00:07:58,765
And a URL isn't a pocket. It's a label on
the outside of the box, read by every tool

108
00:07:58,805 --> 00:08:00,117
that handles the box.

109
00:08:01,957 --> 00:08:06,518
Keep this box for the demo. It describes
a surprise you'll meet while repairing.

110
00:08:06,858 --> 00:08:09,845
One line of configuration,
and three ways out of it.

111
00:08:09,885 --> 00:08:13,745
Two of them are commands that nobody
thinks of as printing secrets.

112
00:08:15,585 --> 00:08:19,232
What an old tutorial tells you
to do. The token here is fake.

113
00:08:19,572 --> 00:08:24,845
Three commands that read configuration. In
how many of them will the token appear:

114
00:08:24,885 --> 00:08:28,084
one, two or all three? Say
it out loud. I'll wait.

115
00:08:32,924 --> 00:08:38,378
In all three. Twice in git remote -v, once
in the file, once in git config list,

116
00:08:38,418 --> 00:08:43,185
where --show-scope tells you it's
local: it's in this repository's own

117
00:08:43,225 --> 00:08:44,146
configuration.

118
00:08:44,486 --> 00:08:49,135
Git itself treats the URL as a credential:
it splits out the username and the

119
00:08:49,175 --> 00:08:51,266
password. That's why the URL works.

120
00:08:51,606 --> 00:08:53,078
Now tell Git to refuse.

121
00:08:53,418 --> 00:08:56,555
"Uses plaintext credentials",
for fetch and for push.

122
00:08:56,595 --> 00:08:59,791
Notice that Git redacts the
secret in its own message.

123
00:09:00,131 --> 00:09:00,733
The repair.

124
00:09:01,073 --> 00:09:04,737
With die in force, we run the
command that would fix the URL.

125
00:09:04,777 --> 00:09:06,861
Does it work? Make your prediction.

126
00:09:11,701 --> 00:09:16,281
git remote set-url refuses, with the
same message: it reads the remote's

127
00:09:16,321 --> 00:09:21,414
configuration before changing it. Almost
everyone is surprised by that the first

128
00:09:21,454 --> 00:09:25,905
time. The way through is to write
the key directly with git config set

129
00:09:25,945 --> 00:09:28,472
remote.origin.url. The URL is now clean.

130
00:09:28,812 --> 00:09:33,514
And the last line of the transcript is
the one to read aloud: the token is still

131
00:09:33,554 --> 00:09:36,894
compromised. It sat in a file
and on a screen. Revoke it.

132
00:09:37,234 --> 00:09:41,626
Try it now, thirty seconds, alone
and not on a shared screen.

133
00:09:41,666 --> 00:09:47,585
In a repository of your own, run git remote
-v and look between "https://" and the

134
00:09:47,625 --> 00:09:49,111
host name. I'll wait.

135
00:09:53,951 --> 00:09:58,479
If there's nothing there, good. If there's
a user, a colon and a string, you know

136
00:09:58,519 --> 00:10:02,146
both halves of the recovery: clean
the URL, and revoke the token.

137
00:10:03,986 --> 00:10:05,634
Five mistakes to watch for.

138
00:10:05,974 --> 00:10:09,855
Putting a token into the remote
URL because a tutorial does.

139
00:10:09,895 --> 00:10:15,150
Root cause: the URL is stored in .git/config
and printed by git remote -v and git

140
00:10:15,190 --> 00:10:19,856
config list; the textbook notes that
the technique is wrong in any year.

141
00:10:20,196 --> 00:10:23,497
Cleaning the URL and considering
the incident closed.

142
00:10:23,537 --> 00:10:28,730
Root cause: erasing is not revoking; the
token stays valid until GitHub revokes it.

143
00:10:29,070 --> 00:10:34,425
Using a classic token with repo scope for
a bot. Root cause: a classic token reaches

144
00:10:34,465 --> 00:10:39,756
every repository and organization its creator
can, so the blast radius is a person.

145
00:10:40,096 --> 00:10:43,581
Choosing a fine-grained token
for docker login ghcr.io.

146
00:10:43,621 --> 00:10:48,323
Root cause: Packages is one of the
documented gaps of fine-grained tokens.

147
00:10:48,663 --> 00:10:53,567
Validating tokens in code by length or
pattern. Root cause: formats change;

148
00:10:53,607 --> 00:10:57,655
installation tokens moved to a
format of about 520 characters.

149
00:10:59,495 --> 00:11:03,002
Now, out of the lab. The
changelog bot from the hook.

150
00:11:03,042 --> 00:11:08,490
The prefix in the ticket is ghp_: a classic
token. With repo scope it can read and

151
00:11:08,530 --> 00:11:13,175
write every private repository its
creator can, in every organization.

152
00:11:13,215 --> 00:11:18,328
That's how far it reaches. When it leaks
through a log, the blast radius is a

153
00:11:18,368 --> 00:11:23,213
person, not a bot. And since the person
has left, nobody was watching it.

154
00:11:23,553 --> 00:11:28,800
The response, in order. Revoke the token.
Clean the URL in the job's checkout with

155
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:34,152
git config set remote.origin.url. Set
transfer.credentialsInUrl to die on the build

156
00:11:34,192 --> 00:11:38,020
machines. And find the other
places the same token was used.

157
00:11:38,060 --> 00:11:43,307
Then replace the design. The same job with
an installation token leaks one hour of

158
00:11:43,347 --> 00:11:48,594
access to one repository. That's the
difference the table in this video describes,

159
00:11:48,634 --> 00:11:50,141
measured in an incident.

160
00:11:51,981 --> 00:11:57,936
Your turn. Do Exercise 20.2, "Pick the
credential", in exercises/m19-m25-github.md.

161
00:11:57,976 --> 00:12:03,858
For each situation, decide on the credential
type before you look anything up, and

162
00:12:03,898 --> 00:12:09,564
write down the two properties that decided
it: what it reaches, and how long it

163
00:12:09,604 --> 00:12:09,997
lives.

164
00:12:10,337 --> 00:12:15,499
The challenge is Exercise 20.7, "The
nightly sync says 'not found'", in the same

165
00:12:15,539 --> 00:12:15,824
file.

166
00:12:17,664 --> 00:12:20,535
Question 310 of the CTO question bank:

167
00:12:22,875 --> 00:12:27,746
Answer out loud. A strong answer starts
with where a remote URL is stored and who

168
00:12:27,786 --> 00:12:32,839
reads it. It lists four concrete places,
names the setting with its three values and

169
00:12:32,879 --> 00:12:36,173
the version it needs, and
mentions the repair surprise.

170
00:12:36,213 --> 00:12:39,568
It finishes with the step that
cleaning doesn't replace.

171
00:12:41,408 --> 00:12:42,336
Let's land this.

172
00:12:42,676 --> 00:12:44,336
You should now be able to say:

173
00:12:44,676 --> 00:12:49,368
The prefix identifies the kind of token,
and the kind decides reach and lifetime.

174
00:12:49,708 --> 00:12:54,452
A classic token reaches everything its
user can; a fine-grained token reaches one

175
00:12:54,492 --> 00:12:58,468
owner with chosen permissions; an
installation token lives one hour.

176
00:12:58,808 --> 00:13:03,691
Fine-grained tokens have documented gaps,
among them public repositories where you

177
00:13:03,731 --> 00:13:07,414
are not a member, several
organizations at once, and Packages.

178
00:13:07,754 --> 00:13:13,390
A token in a URL lands in .git/config, in
git remote -v, in git config list, and

179
00:13:13,430 --> 00:13:15,589
from there in logs and backups.

180
00:13:15,929 --> 00:13:21,974
transfer.credentialsInUrl=die refuses such
URLs; the repair is to clean the URL and

181
00:13:22,014 --> 00:13:23,220
revoke the token.

182
00:13:25,060 --> 00:13:29,413
Read sections 16.6 and 16.7 of Chapter 16.

183
00:13:29,753 --> 00:13:34,665
Today you learned to size up a leaked token
from its first characters, and to clean

184
00:13:34,705 --> 00:13:39,618
a URL without mistaking that for the fix.
Next time: SSH key pairs, the agent, host

185
00:13:39,658 --> 00:13:44,272
keys, and testing the connection. Until
then, look at the state first and type

186
00:13:44,312 --> 00:13:46,182
second. See you in the next one.
